1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:08,950
Here's what Conclave does with a conversation, between the moment you send a message and the moment the model replies.

2
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:19,450
It starts with a log. Every message, document and tool result is written to an append-only SQLite record before anything else happens.

3
00:00:19,700 --> 00:00:26,150
Each entry gets an event ID. Nothing in this log is ever edited or deleted.

4
00:00:27,400 --> 00:00:33,850
Conclave groups the log into context bundles. Each bundle remembers which events it came from.

5
00:00:34,100 --> 00:00:46,150
Some bundles are protected. Named task state, like the fixed budget and the irrigation decision, is outlined in green. Pins are pink. The four most recent turns are amber.

6
00:00:46,400 --> 00:00:51,250
Everything else is an ordinary bundle that can be reshaped later.

7
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,550
Now the split. Each bundle is copied out of the log, and the log itself slides to the left.

8
00:01:00,800 --> 00:01:14,450
On the left is the trajectory: the full record, exactly as it was. On the right is the live context, a versioned projection of what the model will see on its next call.

9
00:01:15,700 --> 00:01:21,750
Then a new question arrives: what did the survey say about the east fence?

10
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:30,450
It's logged first, then added to the live context as the current request. The recent window slides forward by one.

11
00:01:30,700 --> 00:01:40,950
The live context has a byte budget, with room held back for the model's answer. This next request would use 86% of it.

12
00:01:41,200 --> 00:01:53,450
Past 75%, Conclave manages attention before it answers. Pins, named state and the last four bundles, including your question, are locked. The five older, ordinary bundles are candidates.

13
00:01:54,700 --> 00:02:08,750
Deciding what to do with each candidate is a small, frequent judgment. Conclave can hand that job to Jev, a typed decision model from TypeSafe, and keep the main model for the hard work.

14
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:22,250
Jev receives the task and at most six candidates. Each is a short excerpt with its type, its status, and whether a pointer would make it smaller. Protected bundles are never sent.

15
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:34,950
For every candidate, Jev answers two typed questions. Which action: retain, offload, compact or escalate. And how important it is, on a scale from 0 to 4.

16
00:02:35,200 --> 00:02:40,050
Each answer comes back as a probability distribution with a confidence.

17
00:02:40,300 --> 00:02:55,550
Then code checks every answer. The vendor quotes came back at 58% confidence, below the 65% floor, so they are escalated instead. Escalated and retained bundles stay protected for the rest of this turn.

18
00:02:55,800 --> 00:03:09,050
Jev only chooses. It can't write summaries, invent bundle IDs or delete sources. If its answer is malformed, fails, or refers to a stale revision, Conclave falls back to its own ranking.

19
00:03:10,300 --> 00:03:22,950
Now the plan is applied. The site survey becomes a small pointer to its original in the log. That alone leaves the request at 77%, so Conclave keeps going.

20
00:03:23,200 --> 00:03:31,250
The two older assistant turns are compacted. The main model rewrites them into one summary that cites both sources.

21
00:03:31,500 --> 00:03:43,350
The rewrite is kept only if it shrinks the projection by at least 15%. This one roughly halves it. Every step left a receipt in the log.

22
00:03:44,600 --> 00:03:53,850
With the context back under budget, the answer request is assembled: instructions, tools, and the current projection, which ends with your question.

23
00:03:54,100 --> 00:04:00,550
Before anything is sent, the request plus the output reserve is checked against the budget.

24
00:04:01,800 --> 00:04:09,050
The survey is only a pointer now, so the model calls a tool to read the original.

25
00:04:09,300 --> 00:04:15,750
A bounded excerpt comes back from the log and is added to this request only.

26
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:25,250
The model answers. The answer joins the live context, the excerpt stays in the log, and the recent window slides forward again.

27
00:04:26,500 --> 00:04:38,150
So Conclave keeps two records. The trajectory is permanent and complete. The live context is small, rebuilt for every call, and can be restored to any earlier revision.

28
00:04:38,400 --> 00:04:45,250
Summaries and pointers keep their source IDs, so an original is always one tool call away.
